To add an element to a BurstQueue, you use the `Enqueue` method. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt;

NativeArray<int> allocator = Allocator.Temp;
BurstQueue<int> queue = new BurstQueue<int>(allocator);
queue.Enqueue(42);
queue.Dispose();
allocator.Dispose();
```

Unfortunately, the `BurstQueue` struct does not provide a direct method to check if an element is in the queue. However, you can iterate through the queue to find the element. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt;

NativeArray<int> allocator = Allocator.Temp;
BurstQueue<int> queue = new BurstQueue<int>(allocator);
queue.Enqueue(42);

bool containsElement = false;
foreach (int item in queue)
{
    if (item == 42)
    {
        containsElement = true;
        break;
    }
}

queue.Dispose();
allocator.Dispose();
```

To get the number of elements in a BurstQueue, you can access the `Count` property. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt;

NativeArray<int> allocator = Allocator.Temp;
BurstQueue<int> queue = new BurstQueue<int>(allocator);
queue.Enqueue(42);

int count = queue.Count;

queue.Dispose();
allocator.Dispose();
```

To add a cooldown to a GameObject, you need to add the `CooldownAuthoring` component to it. This component will automatically handle the lifecycle of the cooldown. In a system, you can check if the cooldown is active and get the remaining time like this:
```csharp
if (SystemAPI.IsComponentEnabled<Cooldown>(entity))
{
    var cooldown = SystemAPI.GetComponent<Cooldown>(entity);
    float remaining = cooldown.time;
}
```

To create a custom attribute in the CyberEntity Core package, you need to create a new attribute class that inherits from `PropertyAttribute`. You can then use this attribute in your components. For example, to create a custom attribute called `MyCustomAttribute`, you can do the following:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

namespace CyberEntt.Attributes
{
    public class MyCustomAttribute : PropertyAttribute
    {
        public string message;

        public MyCustomAttribute(string message)
        {
            this.message = message;
        }
    }
}
```

To implement a singleton pattern for a MonoBehaviour, you can use the `Singleton<T>` class provided by CyberEntity Core. Here is an example of how to use it:

```csharp
public class MySingleton : Singleton<MySingleton>
{
    // Your singleton code here
}
```

To initialize the deferred logging buffer with a custom capacity, you can call the `DeferredLog.Initialize` method with the desired capacity in bytes. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Logging;

void InitializeLoggingBuffer()
{
    DeferredLog.Initialize(2 * 1024 * 1024); // 2 MB capacity
}
```

To check if the deferred log buffer has been allocated, you can use the `DeferredLog.IsCreated` property. This property returns `true` if the buffer has been allocated, and `false` otherwise. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Logging;

void CheckBufferStatus()
{
    if (DeferredLog.IsCreated)
    {
        Debug.Log("Deferred log buffer is created.");
    }
    else
    {
        Debug.Log("Deferred log buffer is not created.");
    }
}
```

To ensure that deferred logging is properly initialized and released, you should call `DeferredLog.Retain` in the `OnCreate` method and `DeferredLog.Release` in the `OnDestroy` method of your `DeferredLoggingSystem`. Here's a complete example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Logging;
using Unity.Entities;

public partial struct MySystem : ISystem, ISystemStartStop
{
    public void OnCreate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        DeferredLog.Retain();
        Log.Verbose("MySystem created.", ref state);
    }

    public void OnStartRunning(ref SystemState state)
    {
        Log.Verbose("MySystem started.", ref state);
    }

    public void OnStopRunning(ref SystemState state)
    {
        Log.Verbose("MySystem stopped.");
    }

    public void OnDestroy(ref SystemState state)
    {
        Log.Verbose("MySystem destroyed.");
        DeferredLog.Release();
    }

    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // System logic here
    }
}
```

To log a message in a Unity ECS system, you can use the `Log.Info` method. Here's a short example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Logging;

public class MySystem : SystemBase
{
    protected override void OnUpdate()
    {
        Log.Info(this, "This is an info message from MySystem");
    }
}
```

To check if a specific world filter exists in the logging system, you can use the `GetOrCreateWorldFilter` method. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Logging;

public class MySystem : SystemBase
{
    protected override void OnUpdate()
    {
        var filter = Log.GetOrCreateWorldFilter("MyWorld");
        if (filter != null)
        {
            Log.Info(this, "World filter exists");
        }
        else
        {
            Log.Info(this, "World filter does not exist");
        }
    }
}
```

To add a new filter, you need to call the `DrawListFooterButtons` method and pass the serialized filter list property, a reference to the selected index, and a label. The method will handle adding a new filter with default values. Here's a short example:

```csharp
SerializedProperty filterList = serializedObject.FindProperty("filterList");
int selectedIndex = -1;
DrawListFooterButtons(filterList, ref selectedIndex, "MyFilter");
```
This code assumes you have a serialized property named `filterList` in your script and a method to call `DrawListFooterButtons`.

To set the active tab to 'Worlds' when opening the window, you can modify the `Open` method to set the `activeTabName` variable to 'Worlds' before calling `GetWindow<MainWindow>()`. Here is an example:

```csharp
[MenuItem("Window/CyberEntity/Logging")]
public static void Open()
{
    var editorAssembly = typeof(EditorWindow).Assembly;
    var inspectorWindowType = editorAssembly.GetType("UnityEditor.InspectorWindow");

    var window = inspectorWindowType == null ? GetWindow<MainWindow>() :
        GetWindow<MainWindow>("Logging", inspectorWindowType);

    window.activeTabName = "Worlds";
    window.titleContent = new GUIContent("Logging");
    window.Show();
}
```

The `RegisterValueChangedCallback` method registers a callback that is called when the value of a control changes. The callback is passed an event object containing the new value. Here's an example of how to use it:

```csharp
searchBox.RegisterValueChangedCallback(evt =>
{
    _worldsSearch = evt.newValue;
    BuildFilterTab(false);
});
```

To create a settings row for a boolean property, you can use the `CreateSettingsRow` method. Here's a short example:

```csharp
var myBoolField = new Toggle();
myBoolField.Init(false);
var mySettingsRow = CreateSettingsRow(
    "My Bool",
    "Description of my boolean setting",
    myBoolField,
    nameof(MySettingsProperties.myBool)
);
```
This will create a settings row with a toggle field bound to the `myBool` property in `MySettingsProperties`.

To add a new settings card for a custom property, you can create a new method that follows the pattern of existing card creation methods. Here's an example for a custom property `myCustomProperty`:

```csharp
private VisualElement CreateCustomSettingsCard()
{
    var card = CreateSettingsCard("Custom Settings");
    var myCustomField = new TextField();
    myCustomField.Init(_serializedSettings.FindProperty(nameof(MySettingsProperties.myCustomProperty)).stringValue);
    card.Add(CreateSettingsRow(
        "Custom Property",
        "Description of my custom property",
        myCustomField,
        nameof(MySettingsProperties.myCustomProperty)
    ));
    return card;
}
```
Then, add this card to the scroll view in the `InitializeUI` method.

To initialize the `EnumField` for the logging level with a default value of `Verbose`, you can use the `Init` method of the `EnumField` class. Here's an example:

```csharp
var levelField = new EnumField();
levelField.Init(Level.Verbose);
```
This sets the default value of the `EnumField` to `Verbose`, which is the first value in the `Level` enum.

To log an exception, you can use the `Log.Exception` method. Here is an example:

```csharp
try
{
    // Some code that might throw an exception
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    Log.Exception(ex);
}
```

To use the `CountThingsAndDoStuff` method, you need to create an instance of `MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass` and call the method with the required parameters. Here's a simple example:

```csharp
using Lmartinignacio.Netcode;

public class ExampleUsage : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass example = new MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass();
        int result = example.CountThingsAndDoStuff(5, 3, true);
        Debug.Log(result); // Outputs: 8
    }
}
```

You can create a new method that takes two lists of integers and uses the `CountThingsAndDoStuff` method to calculate the total number of items in both lists. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Lmartinignacio.Netcode;

public class ItemCounter : MonoBehaviour
{
    public List<int> List1;
    public List<int> List2;

    void Start()
    {
        MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass example = new MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass();
        int totalItems = example.CountThingsAndDoStuff(List1.Count, List2.Count, true);
        Debug.Log(totalItems); // Outputs the total number of items in both lists
    }
}
```

To instantiate the `MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass` class in a Unity script, you simply need to create a new instance of the class. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Lmartinignacio.Netcode;

public class ExampleUsage : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass example = new MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass();
    }
}
```

To call the `CountThingsAndDoStuffAndOutputIt` method, you need to create an instance of `MyPublicSampleExampleClass` and then invoke the method on that instance. Here's a simple example in your Unity project:

```csharp
using Lmartinignacio.Netcode;

public class ExampleUsage : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        MyPublicSampleExampleClass example = new MyPublicSampleExampleClass();
        example.CountThingsAndDoStuffAndOutputIt();
    }
}
```

To modify the `CountThingsAndDoStuffAndOutputIt` method to pass different parameters, you can change the arguments when calling `CountThingsAndDoStuff`. For example, to pass integers 3 and 4 and a boolean true, you would modify the method as follows:

```csharp
public void CountThingsAndDoStuffAndOutputIt()
{
    var result = new MyPublicRuntimeExampleClass().CountThingsAndDoStuff(3, 4, true);
    Debug.Log("Call CountThingsAndDoStuffAndOutputIt returns " + result);
}
```

To use the `MyPublicSampleExampleClass` in a script attached to a GameObject, you need to create an instance of the class in the script and call its methods as needed. Here's an example of how you can do this:

```csharp
using Lmartinignacio.Netcode;

public class ExampleScript : MonoBehaviour
{
    private MyPublicSampleExampleClass example;

    void Start()
    {
        example = new MyPublicSampleExampleClass();
        example.CountThingsAndDoStuffAndOutputIt();
    }
}
```

To add a boolean toggle setting for 'LocalSimulation', you can use the `AddBoolSetting` method. Here's an example of how to do it:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Preferences;

public class MyEditorWindow : EditorWindow
{
    [MenuItem("Window/My Editor Window")] static void Init()
    {
        GetWindow<MyEditorWindow>("My Editor Window").Show();
    }

    void OnGUI()
    {
        EditorPreferences.AddBoolSetting(PreferenceKey.LocalSimulation.ToString(), "Enable Local Simulation", false, "Enable or disable local simulation mode.");
    }
}
```

To retrieve the current value of the 'LocalSimulation' preference, you can use the `EditorPrefs.GetBool` method. Here's an example of how to do it:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Preferences;

public class MyScript : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        bool localSimulationEnabled = EditorPrefs.GetBool(PreferenceKey.LocalSimulation.ToString(), false);
        Debug.Log("Local Simulation Enabled: " + localSimulationEnabled);
    }
}
```

To use the `MonoSingleton<T>` class, create a subclass that inherits from it. For example, to create a singleton for a `PlayerController`, you would do the following:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Singleton;

public class PlayerController : MonoSingleton<PlayerController>
{
    // Your singleton code here
}
```

To ensure a GameObject is destroyed when its scene is unloaded, you can use the `DontDestroyOnLoad` method in the `Awake` method of your singleton. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.Singleton;

public class PersistentObject : MonoSingleton<PersistentObject>
{
    protected override void Awake()
    {
        base.Awake();
        DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject);
    }
}
```

To send a PhysicalDamageRPC from a client to the server, you need to create an instance of the PhysicalDamageRPC struct and call the Send method on the NetworkBehaviour component. Here's a short example:
```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;

public class DamageSender : NetworkBehaviour
{
    public void SendDamage(float value, int source, int target)
    {
        var rpc = new PhysicalDamageRPC { Value = value, Source = source, Target = target };
        SendRpcCommand(rpc);
    }
}
```

To handle a PhysicalDamageRPC on the server side, you need to create a method that takes a PhysicalDamageRPC parameter and applies the damage to the target entity. Here's an example:
```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;

public class DamageHandler : NetworkBehaviour
{
    [ServerRpc]
    public void HandlePhysicalDamage(PhysicalDamageRPC rpc)
    {
        var targetEntity = GetEntityFromId(rpc.Target);
        if (targetEntity != null)
        {
            ApplyDamage(targetEntity, rpc.Value);
        }
    }

    private void ApplyDamage(Entity entity, float damage)
    {
        // Logic to apply damage to the entity
    }
}
```

To receive a PhysicalDamageRPC on the client side, you need to create a method that takes a PhysicalDamageRPC parameter and applies the damage to the target entity. This method should be decorated with the [ClientRpc] attribute. Here's an example:
```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;

public class DamageReceiver : NetworkBehaviour
{
    [ClientRpc]
    public void ReceivePhysicalDamage(PhysicalDamageRPC rpc)
    {
        var targetEntity = GetEntityFromId(rpc.Target);
        if (targetEntity != null)
        {
            ApplyDamage(targetEntity, rpc.Value);
        }
    }

    private void ApplyDamage(Entity entity, float damage)
    {
        // Logic to apply damage to the entity
    }
}
```

To add the InputAuthoring component to a GameObject, you can use the AddComponent method in your script. Here's a short example:

```csharp
GameObject myGameObject = new GameObject();
myGameObject.AddComponent<CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components.InputAuthoring>();
```

To enable the FreeMovementData component for a ghost entity, you need to add it to the entity's component bag. Here's a short example in C#:

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.NetCode;

void EnableFreeMovementData(Entity entity)
{
    EntityManager entityManager = World.DefaultGameObjectInjectionWorld.EntityManager;
    entityManager.AddComponentData(entity, new FreeMovementData());
}
```

To check if a ghost entity has the DiagonalNotAllowedTag component, you can use the HasComponent method of the EntityManager. Here's an example in C#:

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.NetCode;

bool HasDiagonalNotAllowedTag(Entity entity)
{
    EntityManager entityManager = World.DefaultGameObjectInjectionWorld.EntityManager;
    return entityManager.HasComponent<DiagonalNotAllowedTag>(entity);
}
```

To disable the UnitBasedMovementData component for a ghost entity, you need to remove it from the entity's component bag. Here's a short example in C#:

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.NetCode;

void DisableUnitBasedMovementData(Entity entity)
{
    EntityManager entityManager = World.DefaultGameObjectInjectionWorld.EntityManager;
    entityManager.RemoveComponent<UnitBasedMovementData>(entity);
}
```

To set the player name, you need to create an instance of the PlayerNameAuthoring component and assign the desired player name to the `playerName` field. Here's a short example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;

PlayerNameAuthoring authoring = gameObject.AddComponent<PlayerNameAuthoring>();
authoring.playerName = "JohnDoe";
```

To update the player name when it changes in the UI, you can listen for a change event on the TMP_Text component and update the `playerName` field of the PlayerNameAuthoring component. Here's an example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;
using TMPro;

public class PlayerNameUpdater : MonoBehaviour
{
    public TMP_Text playerNameText;
    private PlayerNameAuthoring authoring;

    void Start()
    {
        authoring = GetComponent<PlayerNameAuthoring>();
        playerNameText.onValueChanged.AddListener(UpdatePlayerName);
    }

    void UpdatePlayerName(string newName)
    {
        authoring.playerName = newName;
    }
}
```

To retrieve the player name from a player entity, you can use the `GetComponent` method on the entity to get the `PlayerName` component. Here's a short example:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;

Entity playerEntity = ...; // Get the player entity
PlayerName playerNameComponent = playerEntity.GetComponent<PlayerName>();
string playerName = playerNameComponent.value;
```

To enable collision checking for an entity, you need to add the `CollisionCheckTag` component to the entity. Here's a short example of how to do it in C#:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;
using Unity.Entities;

Entity entity = ...; // Get the entity you want to enable collision checking for
EntityManager entityManager = ...; // Get the EntityManager

entityManager.AddComponent(entity, new CollisionCheckTag());
```

To enable the TranslationSystem in a Unity project, you need to add it to your world. Here's a short example of how to do it in C#:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Systems.Movement;
using Unity.Entities;

public class TranslationSystemEnabler : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        var world = World.DefaultGameObjectInjectionWorld;
        world.CreateSystem<TranslationSystem>();
    }
}
```

To set the `NextPosition` of an entity in the TranslationSystem, you need to modify the `MovementData` component. Here's an example of how to do it in C#:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;
using Unity.Entities;

public class SetNextPosition : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Update()
    {
        var entityManager = World.DefaultGameObjectInjectionWorld.EntityManager;
        var movementDataLookup = entityManager.GetComponentLookup<MovementData>();

        foreach (var entity in entityManager.GetAllEntitiesQuery<MovementData>())
        {
            movementDataLookup[entity].NextPosition = new float3(10, 0, 0);
        }
    }
}
```

To create a new entity with the `FreeMovementData` component enabled, you can use the following code:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Shared.Components;
using Unity.Entities;

Entity newEntity = entityManager.CreateEntity(typeof(FreeMovementData));
```

To connect to a server, you need to create an instance of `ClientSystem` and call its `OnStartRunning` method. This method sets up a connection request to the server at the specified endpoint. Here's a simple example of how to do it in a Unity script:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Client.Systems;

public class ConnectToServer : MonoBehaviour
{
    private ClientSystem clientSystem;

    void Start()
    {
        clientSystem = new ClientSystem();
        clientSystem.OnStartRunning(ref new SystemState());
    }
}
```

To disconnect from the server, you need to call the `OnDestroy` method of the `ClientSystem` class. This method will check if the client is currently connected and, if so, send a disconnection request. Here's a simple example of how to do it in a Unity script:

```csharp
using CyberEntt.NetCode.Client.Systems;

public class DisconnectFromServer : MonoBehaviour
{
    private ClientSystem clientSystem;

    void OnDestroy()
    {
        if (clientSystem != null)
        {
            clientSystem.OnDestroy(ref new SystemState());
        }
    }
}
```

To update the Move and Look values in the InputData component for a specific entity, you can use the following code snippet:
```csharp
var entity = GetSingletonEntity<GhostOwnerIsLocal>();
var inputData = GetComponentRW<InputData>(entity);
inputData.ValueRW.Move = new Vector2(1.0f, 0.0f);
